{"id":"decision-example-001","type":"decision","fact":"Example: Chose Zustand over Redux for client state management because of simpler API and smaller bundle size.","recommendation":"Use Zustand stores for client state. Use TanStack Query for server state. Do not mix the two patterns.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"human","reference":"Architecture decision","date":"2026-01-09"}],"tags":["architecture","state-management","zustand"],"affectedFiles":["src/stores/**/*.ts"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"decision-posix-pty-firewall-001","type":"decision","fact":"Only PureClaw.Backend.Pty and PureClaw.Backend.Pty.Fake may import System.Posix.Pty (or the posix-pty package). The firewall is enforced by scripts/check-pty-firewall.sh, wired into .githooks/pre-push and .git/hooks/pre-push. This keeps the upstream-dormant posix-pty dependency contained to two modules so it can be replaced as a single unit.","recommendation":"Do NOT add 'posix-pty' or 'System.Posix.Pty' imports outside src/PureClaw/Backend/Pty.hs and src/PureClaw/Backend/Pty/Fake.hs. Any factory that needs PTY I/O takes a PtyIO record as a parameter and delegates. The pre-push hook will block any violation.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"feat/terminal-backends-design","reference":"WU7 PtyIO seam + CI gate","date":"2026-05-14"}],"tags":["architecture","dependency-firewall","backend","security"],"affectedFiles":["src/PureClaw/Backend/Pty.hs","src/PureClaw/Backend/Pty/Fake.hs","scripts/check-pty-firewall.sh",".githooks/pre-push"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-05-14T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-05-14T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"decision-tmux-detach-only-typed-001","type":"decision","fact":"A tmux Attach-mode BackendHandle's close path is type-restricted to detach-only via 'data TmuxCloseAction = TmuxDetach' — a one-constructor sum with the constructor NOT exported. There is no API surface that allows a caller to ask for kill-window / kill-session / kill-pane. Close writes only the Ctrl-B + 'd' key sequence into the existing PTY.","recommendation":"When a destructive operation must be impossible-by-API, encode the permitted action set as a closed sum type with the constructors NOT exported. The close path takes a value of that type; downstream code can only construct the values exported by the module. This is stronger than 'don't call kill-window' as a code-review rule.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"feat/terminal-backends-design","reference":"WU10 polish commit","date":"2026-05-14"}],"tags":["security","type-safety","tmux","api-design"],"affectedFiles":["src/PureClaw/Backend/Tmux.hs"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-05-14T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-05-14T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"decision-allowlist-loud-default-001","type":"decision","fact":"Remote channels (Signal, Telegram) resolve missing/empty allow_from AND dm_policy=\"open\" to AllowAll. A channel resolving to AllowAll must emit a prominent startup warning (multi-line banner to stdout + WARN to stderr), never silently accept everyone. The warning is wired at the activation boundary: Signal in runChat's \"signal\" branch before the signal-cli check (so it fires even on CLI fallback); Telegram in withTelegramChannel.","recommendation":"When adding a channel, resolve its allow-list and call warnIfOpenAllowList at the activation point. Never let an open allow-list be silent.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"agent","reference":"PR #73","date":"2026-05-31"}],"tags":["security","channels","allow-list"],"affectedFiles":["src/PureClaw/CLI/Commands.hs","src/PureClaw/Channels/Telegram.hs"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-05-31T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-05-31T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"decision-telegram-allowlist-dormant-001","type":"decision","fact":"Telegram has no live boot path (webhook-driven; gateway out of scope), so withTelegramChannel and resolveTelegramConfig are fully unit-tested but production-dormant — no production caller until the gateway boot path is built. The effectiveChannel switch in runChat still handles only \"signal\"/\"cli\". Accepted YAGNI tradeoff: the seams mirror the Signal equivalents so the gateway inherits enforcement+warning with no rework.","recommendation":"When wiring the Telegram gateway boot path, route through withTelegramChannel so the allow-list warning and enforcement activate automatically.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"agent","reference":"PR #73","date":"2026-05-31"}],"tags":["channels","telegram","architecture"],"affectedFiles":["src/PureClaw/Channels/Telegram.hs","src/PureClaw/CLI/Commands.hs"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-05-31T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-05-31T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id": "decision-harness-coords-in-tmuxconfig-001", "type": "decision", "fact": "Frontend harness sessions persist their tmux coordinates in the EXISTING _sm_kind HarnessSpec._h_backend = TbTmux TmuxConfig (_tc_window = harnessKey) rather than adding a new SessionMeta field.", "recommendation": "Reuse TmuxConfig for harness coordinates \u2014 it already serializes (Kind.hs) and avoids touching the ~39 SessionMeta construction/match sites; it also aligns with the deferred spawn-vs-attach work (pureclaw-jlc) which will read the same coordinates.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "epic pureclaw-99a", "date": "2026-06-01"}], "tags": ["sessions", "harness", "tmux", "serialization"], "affectedFiles": ["src/PureClaw/Session/Kind.hs", "src/PureClaw/Frontend/API.hs"], "affectedServices": ["SessionMeta"], "createdAt": "2026-06-01T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-06-01T00:00:00Z", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "decision-additive-harness-registry-001", "type": "decision", "fact": "Harness identity moved to a HarnessId TVar registry (PureClaw.Harness.Registry) that is the source of truth, while the legacy Text-keyed _env_harnesses/_fe_harnesses map and _env_nextWindowIdx are KEPT as synced derived views (a spawn inserts into BOTH). Only frontend routing migrated to id-based; full legacy-consumer cutover (/status,/target,/msg, Tab/Harness.lookupHarness, Loop.TargetHarness, resolveResumedTarget) is deferred.", "recommendation": "When replacing a pervasive keyed-map with a richer registry, do it ADDITIVELY: new registry as truth + keep the old map as a synced view, migrate only the highest-value consumer first, defer the rest. Bounds blast radius and keeps every work unit green.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "epic pureclaw-3oy (Harness Registry Phase 1)", "date": "2026-06-01"}], "tags": ["harness", "registry", "architecture", "additive"], "affectedFiles": ["src/PureClaw/Harness/Registry.hs", "src/PureClaw/Agent/Env.hs", "src/PureClaw/Frontend/API.hs"], "affectedServices": ["HarnessRegistry"], "createdAt": "2026-06-02T03:03:45.298808+00:00", "updatedAt": "2026-06-02T03:03:45.298808+00:00", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "decision-id-primary-name-fallback-routing-001", "type": "decision", "fact": "Frontend harness routing is id-primary with name fallback: harnessKeyFromKind returns the persisted HarnessId text when _h_harnessId is Just, else the dual-written _tc_window name; sendToHarness parses the key \u2014 a UUID resolves through the registry (only if PID-corroborated) and otherwise falls back to the legacy name-keyed _fe_harnesses map.", "recommendation": "Make a routing migration self-describing via the key shape (UUID vs name) so a not-yet-populated registry transparently falls back to the prior path \u2014 preserves shipped behavior (PR #74) while the new path activates incrementally.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "epic pureclaw-3oy (Harness Registry Phase 1)", "date": "2026-06-01"}], "tags": ["harness", "routing", "frontend", "migration"], "affectedFiles": ["src/PureClaw/Frontend/API.hs", "src/PureClaw/Session/Kind.hs"], "affectedServices": ["FrontendEnv"], "createdAt": "2026-06-02T03:03:45.298808+00:00", "updatedAt": "2026-06-02T03:03:45.298808+00:00", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "decision-orphan-grace-tick-counter-001", "type": "decision", "fact": "Harness orphan retention/grace is a consecutive-orphaned-tick counter (_he_orphanedTicks, no wall-clock) on the registry entry: increment when Orphaned, reset when live, and the HELD-STALE path (transient sweep/capture failure) HOLDS it (neither advances nor resets) so a flaky tmux can't evict a live harness. At defaultOrphanGraceTicks the entry is auto-evicted from the registry AND the legacy map but session.json is NEVER touched (the session reappears in Recent Sessions \u2014 appears in exactly one section). Manual Dismiss is the same eviction, user-initiated.", "recommendation": "For ephemeral-entity retention, prefer a tick counter over wall-clock (deterministic + test-injectable); make the stale/unknown path HOLD the counter to avoid flap-eviction; never delete the durable record (session.json) on eviction \u2014 only the ephemeral live entry.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "epic pureclaw-3oy (Harness Registry Phase 2)", "date": "2026-06-02"}], "tags": ["harness", "reconcile", "eviction", "lifecycle"], "affectedFiles": ["src/PureClaw/Harness/Reconcile.hs", "src/PureClaw/Harness/Registry.hs"], "affectedServices": ["HarnessRegistry"], "createdAt": "2026-06-02T17:06:30.577980+00:00", "updatedAt": "2026-06-02T17:06:30.577980+00:00", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "decision-typed-default-deny-adoption-gate-001", "type": "decision", "fact": "tmux-window adoption (a trust-boundary expansion) is gated by a typed, default-deny, consent-gated capability: AdoptedHarness (value ctor UNEXPORTED, mirrors AuthorizedCommand \u2014 even coerce is blocked since the Coercible instance is module-private), constructible only via authorizeAdoption which checks consent FIRST (ConsentHeadless dominates) then a default-empty session-pattern allow-list. The dangerous op (adoptExternalWindow) REQUIRES the token as a parameter, so bypassing the gate is impossible by construction. ConsentChannel is threaded from the invocation mode (CmdTui=Interactive, everything else incl gateway/headless=ConsentHeadless, fail-closed).", "recommendation": "For a trust-boundary capability, make the dangerous function REQUIRE an unforgeable token (unexported ctor) produced only by the policy gate; default-deny; check the strongest denier (no-consent/headless) first; thread the consent/run-mode signal explicitly and fail closed. Enforce server-side at the endpoint BEFORE any mutation, not just in the UI.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "epic pureclaw-3oy (Harness Registry Phase 3 \u2014 adopt external tmux)", "date": "2026-06-03"}], "tags": ["security", "adoption", "capability", "default-deny", "tmux"], "affectedFiles": ["src/PureClaw/Security/Adoption.hs", "src/PureClaw/Security/Policy.hs", "src/PureClaw/Frontend/API.hs"], "affectedServices": ["SecurityPolicy"], "createdAt": "2026-06-03T05:18:43.067377+00:00", "updatedAt": "2026-06-03T05:18:43.067377+00:00", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
